---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="OKC Atlas — Oklahoma Inc."
  description="Oklahoma City in nine highway-bounded quadrants, filterable across 29 research categories. What is in each part of the city, and where the gaps are."
  sources={['atlas','land']}
  notes={[
    { title: 'On the quantities', text: 'Quantities count named items found in the research, so each is a floor rather than a full census. Nothing is estimated to fill a gap.' },
    { title: 'On the rolled-up figures', text: 'Figures are averaged across the ZIP codes that fall inside a quadrant, and every one shows how many ZIPs it rests on. ZIP boundaries do not follow the highway lines that define the quadrants.' },
    { title: 'On safety grades', text: 'These come from commercial aggregators rather than the Oklahoma City Police Department. The index those services publish alongside a grade is not comparable between grades, so grades drive the ranking.' },
  ]}
>
  <section class="panel panel--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kick">The opportunity map, up close</p>
      <h1 class="mega">Oklahoma City,<br />in nine pieces.</h1>
      <p class="under">
        The figures elsewhere on this site describe four million people at once. This asks
        the same question at street level: what is actually in each part of the city, and
        where is the gap?
      </p>
      <p class="under">
        Pick what you want a lot of, or a little of — large employers, median income,
        schools, parks, transit, shelters — and all nine quadrants line up against it. Or
        start with a quadrant and read everything named inside it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <okc-atlas></okc-atlas>

</Base>

<!-- The atlas is the point of this page, so it loads with it rather than
     on scroll. It is a self-contained custom element with its own shadow
     root; the tokens below are the only thing this site passes into it. -->
<script type="module" src="/okc-atlas.element.js"></script>

<style is:global>
  okc-atlas {
    display: block;
    margin-block: var(--s-6) var(--s-8);

    /* Retuned to the new design system. Structural tones follow the page;
       the two accent slots take validated series colours, so the safety
       grades no longer resolve to an alarm red. */
    --ink: #16181c;
    --ink-soft: #3d434c;
    --paper: #fbfaf7;
    --paper-2: #f4f2ed;
    --card: #ffffff;
    --rule: #b9b3a6;
    --rule-faint: #e2ded6;
    --signal: #a8621b;
    --brick: #ab461a;
    --field: #00805a;
    --r-lg: 8px;
    --r-md: 8px;
    --r-sm: 4px;
  }
</style>
